  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened mechanisms for collective bargaining and dispute resolution</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Decent Work in Supply Chains-Fisheries Sector:
The Namibian fisheries industry is a significant contributor to the country&apos;s economy, constituting about 15% of all exports and employing approximately 16,300 workers. However, it faces challenges related to decent work, including occupational safety and health, skills development, and illegal, unreported, and unregulated fishing, as well as human trafficking and forced labour, on some vessels using Namibian ports. In 2018, Namibia ratified the Work in Fishing Convention, 2007 (No. 188).
In 2021 the International Labour Organization (ILO) launched the Sustainable Supply Chains to Build Forward Better (SSCBFB) project in cooperation with the European Commission intending to address decent work challenges and opportunities in the fisheries supply chain in Namibia. The project aimed to promote decent work in global supply chains as key to the social and economic recovery from the COVID-19 crisis &#x2013; building forward better; to engage stakeholders and promote social dialogue all along the supply chains to keep decent work at the forefront and explore possibilities for a &#x201c;new normal&#x201d; during and after COVID-19 recovery &#x2013; stakeholder engagement; and to support governments, employers, workers and other stakeholders&apos; actions to make decent work a defining framework of the post-COVID era &#x2013; stakeholders&apos; actions.
The project in Namibia enhanced the understanding of decent work challenges and opportunities in the fisheries supply chain among policymakers, employers and workers. Because of the project, they now come together to strengthen inspection of working conditions in the industry and have decided to establish a National Commission on Decent Work in the Namibian Fisheries Sector. Recognizing that workers onshore and at sea face different challenges that need to be managed jointly with all actors involved, the project engaged the Directorate of Labour Services of the Ministry of Labour, Industrial Relations and Employment Cre</narrative>
  </description>
